        timingType: "Chain (The 3.6 FSI utilizes the VR6 architecture with a highly complex timing chain system located at the rear of the engine, right up against the firewall. Over time, the chains are prone to stretching and the plastic chain guides become brittle and break. Because of its rearward placement, replacing the timing chain components is an incredibly labor-intensive 'engine-out' or 'transmission-out' service. It is vital to monitor chain phase adaptation via VCDS and listen for distinct rattling noises on cold starts to prevent catastrophic failure).", injectionType: "Direct Injection (FSI. Fuel is injected directly into the combustion chamber at high pressures. Because no fuel washes over the back of the intake valves, this engine suffers from severe carbon buildup. This carbon choking restricts airflow, causing misfires, rough idling, and significant power loss, necessitating physical walnut blasting of the intake ports every 100,000 km).", engineLifespan: "250,000+ km (The cast-iron VR6 block itself is an incredibly durable, battle-proven design derived from VW's legendary VR6 lineage. The mechanical internals will easily last beyond 300,000 km. However, the vehicle's functional lifespan is often limited by the immense labor costs associated with rear timing chain replacement, catastrophic PCV valve failures blowing rear main seals, and general cooling system degradation).", turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (The 3.6 FSI relies entirely on displacement and its narrow 10.6-degree VR6 cylinder angle to produce power. It features a dual-branch intake manifold with variable runner lengths to optimize low-end torque and high-end horsepower, delivering a very linear, smooth powerband with a distinct, legendary VR6 exhaust note).", ecuType: "Bosch Motronic MED 9.1 (A highly capable engine control unit managing the high-pressure fueling, continuous variable valve timing, and variable intake manifold flaps. As a naturally aspirated engine, software tuning yields very minimal horsepower gains, usually just optimizing throttle response).", euroStandard: "Euro 4", maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The VR6 rotating assembly is phenomenally strong. While naturally aspirated tuning maxes out around 300 hp, the stock internals can easily handle bolt-on supercharger kits running moderate boost, making it a highly robust foundation if forced induction is applied).",
        fuelConsumption: { city: "14.5 - 16.5 L/100km", highway: "9.5 - 11.5 L/100km", mixed: "12.5 - 14.0 L/100km", sporty: "18.0+ L/100km" }, oilSpecification: "VW 504 00 5W-30",
        commonIssues: [ "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The long, rear-mounted timing chains stretch over time, and the hydraulic tensioners lose pressure. Causes extreme cold-start rattling and eventual timing jump.", "PCV Valve Diaphragm Rupture: The Positive Crankcase Ventilation valve membrane tears, causing massive internal vacuum leaks, whistling noises, a rough idle, and severe oil consumption.", "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: The pure FSI direct injection system allows thick, hardened carbon deposits to form on the intake valves, choking the engine of air and causing cold misfires.", "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: While utilizing a more robust roller follower than the 2.0T, the HPFP and its cam lobe can still suffer wear if oil changes are neglected.", "Ignition Coil Pack Failure: The coils are located deep in the narrow VR6 cylinder head and degrade rapidly due to severe heat cycling, leading to ignition misfires under load.", "Variable Intake Manifold Flap Seizure: The plastic flaps inside the intake manifold can bind with carbon or wear out, throwing CEL codes and restricting torque." ],
        vulnerabilities: [ "Catastrophic Valvetrain Destruction - Ignoring a stretched timing chain or failing tensioner will inevitably cause the chain to jump teeth, bending the valves and destroying the cylinder head.", "Rear Main Seal Blowout - If the PCV valve fails and pressurizes the crankcase, the weakest point is the rear main seal. Replacing it requires dropping the transmission.", "Valve Seat Pitting - Severe, unaddressed carbon buildup on the intake valves can eventually cause the valves to improperly seat, leading to permanent pitting and loss of compression.", "Coolant Pipe Cracking - The plastic coolant distribution pipes attached to the block become incredibly brittle over a decade of heat cycling and will shatter without warning.", "Catalytic Converter Failure - Chronic misfires from bad coils or heavy carbon buildup will dump unburnt fuel into the exhaust, melting the expensive catalytic converters.", "09D Valve Body Failure - Neglecting the Aisin automatic transmission fluid leads to severe wear in the valve body, causing harsh, clunky shifts from 1st to 2nd gear." ],
        oemPlusUpgrades: [ { name: "Stage 1 ECU & TCU Remap", description: "Optimization of the Bosch MED 9.1 ECU and 09D transmission controller.", benefit: "While peak horsepower gains are minimal (15-20 hp) on a naturally aspirated engine, the tune drastically improves throttle response, removes limiters, and optimizes shift points for a much more engaging drive." }, { name: "R8 Ignition Coil Upgrade", description: "Installing high-output red ignition coils originally designed for the Audi R8 V10.", benefit: "Provides a significantly more stable and powerful spark, curing common misfires and offering better durability under the extreme heat of the VR6 cylinder head." }, { name: "Billet Aluminum Crack Pipe", description: "Replacing the notorious plastic coolant transfer pipe ('crack pipe') with an aftermarket aluminum version.", benefit: "Permanently solves the most common and catastrophic coolant leak vulnerability on the VR6 architecture." }, { name: "PCV Valve Catch Can System", description: "Installing an external oil catch can to intercept oil vapor before it enters the intake.", benefit: "Significantly reduces the rate of carbon buildup on the intake valves and prevents future PCV diaphragm ruptures." } ],
